{"uid":"modules/mediation/engine_mediator.py","name":"engine_mediator.py","qualname":"modules/mediation/engine_mediator.py","kind":"module","file":"modules/mediation/engine_mediator.py","line":1,"end_line":118,"language":"py","signature":"","params":[],"returns":"","decorators":[],"bases":[],"modifiers":[],"visibility":"public","doc":{"summary":"Engine mediator: the loop's single coordination point to the engines.","description":"Domain ``loop.engines``. The unified autonomous loop (``LazyLoopEngine``)\nand the CLI/MCP exploit surfaces used to import the exploit engines\ndirectly (``modules.autonomous_exploit_engine``,\n``modules.reactive_engine``, ``modules.ai_exploit_chain``), which couples\nthe loop god file to every engine implementation. This mediator owns that\nedge instead: consumers ask for a port, the mediator lazily resolves the\nconcrete engine at call time, so monkeypatching an engine stays effective\nand import cycles die.\n\nPorts:\n\n- :class:`ExploitRetryPort` — feed captured credentials back into the\n  autonomous exploit engine's retry queue (credential reuse).\n- :class:`ReactionPort` — reactive-engine analysis of step output into\n  next-action decisions.\n\nFailure semantics: every port call degrades to a no-op answer (empty\nlist, empty string) when its engine import fails, so a missing engine\nnever breaks the loop round.","params":[],"returns":null,"yields":null,"raises":[],"attributes":[],"examples":[],"notes":[],"see_also":[],"deprecated":"","since":"","style":"plain"},"parent":"","members":["modules/mediation/engine_mediator.py::ExploitRetryPort","modules/mediation/engine_mediator.py::ReactionPort","modules/mediation/engine_mediator.py::EngineMediator"],"inferred_raises":[],"returns_value":null,"value":"","base_uids":[],"subclasses":[],"calls":[],"called_by":[]}
